  "question": "When Sortformer's speech spans gap at pauses, does meeting.AttributeTranscript leave transcript turns inside those gaps unlabelled, or does its overlap rule absorb them?",
  "answer_in_one_line": "On this fixture: absorbed. Every one of the 25 real transcript turns keeps at least 120 ms of overlap with some Sortformer span, so all 25 are labelled and every label is identical to the sherpa control \u2014 but there is no nearest-span fallback, so a turn that falls entirely inside a gap would be dropped to unknown/rejected.",
  "rule_as_written": {
    "file": "internal/meeting/speakers.go",
    "func": "AttributeTranscript",
    "lines": "123-158",
    "file_sha256": "625de03e275c21cb5a15b4344fb033f98597ff18fe5672a8a34fb1ae3435c8f2",
    "repo_commit": "2e44ef48f8effe122a0ff0873ed5b1de7127764a",
    "summary": "Pure greatest-positive-overlap, no threshold, no nearest-span fallback, no tolerance window. For each transcript segment it scans every timeline observation and computes min(segEnd,obsEnd)-max(segStart,obsStart); overlap<=0 is skipped entirely (l.131-134). The strictly-largest overlap wins (l.135-137); an exact tie for the maximum, or a winner whose State is StateOverlap, yields speaker=unknown with State=overlap (l.145-151). If no observation overlaps at all (best<0, l.141-143) the segment keeps its initialised value: Speaker=speaker.LabelUnknown, State=speaker.StateRejected, Confidence=0 \u2014 i.e. it is dropped as unlabelled, never attached to the nearest span.",
    "threshold_ms": 0,
    "nearest_span_fallback": false,
    "unlabelled_representation": {
      "speaker": "unknown",
      "state": "rejected"
    }
  },
  "method": {
    "read_only": "projects/obey-voice was never modified for the measurement; the repo was rsync-copied to a scratchpad and the probe programs were added to the copy (internal/meeting/speakers.go sha256 identical in both). The probes and inputs were subsequently committed to this repo and the attribution + shift sweep re-run in-tree: outputs bit-identical (see raw_files.reproduced).",
    "transcript_segments": "Produced by the shipped WAV->transcript path, not synthesised: cmd/obey-voice/cmd/meeting_serve_pipeline.go newReplaySource + replayTranscribe driving the configured STT provider (parakeet, offline mode) over the fixture, with utterances appended through the production internal/meeting/log.Writer.OnUtterance. That writer is what stamps StartMS/EndMS (log.go:274-308: EndMS = utterance completion offset into the recording, StartMS = EndMS - 350 ms/word floored at 800 ms, clamped forward to the previous utterance end) and its records are exactly what internal/meeting.logAnalysis feeds AttributeTranscript in production.",
    "sherpa_timeline": "obey-voice meeting analyze <fixture> --speakers 2 --json (GOBIN binary, same command as T3): 6 spans, coverage 84560 ms.",
    "sortformer_timeline": "TRANSCRIBE_SORTFORMER_STREAM_PRESET=very_high_latency transcribe-cli -m diar_streaming_sortformer_4spk-v2.1-Q8_0.gguf --backend metal -q --batch files.txt --batch-jsonl (pinned v0.2.2 CLI): 25 spans, coverage 70240 ms \u2014 reproduces T3's very_high_latency/metal run exactly.",
    "sortformer_to_timeline_mapping": "Each JSONL span became a speaker.Observation with Label=speaker-<speaker_id>, State=stable, Source=recording, Confidence=0 \u2014 the same shape SherpaEngine.Diarize emits, so the only variable between the two runs is span geometry.",
    "attribution": "The real meeting.AttributeTranscript was called on both timelines (cmd/obey-voice/cmd/t3b_attr_test.go, env-gated: skips unless T3B_* are set); no logic was reimplemented.",
